    "narrative": "Today\u2019s optimal lineup for Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily into favorable matchups against mid-tier arms, headlined by **James Wood** (78.42) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91). **James Wood** leads the charge against J.T. Ginn, carrying an elite 0.437 xwOBA and a prime leadoff spot that maximizes his run-scoring potential. **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) benefit significantly from the \"SP Skill\" boost\u2014both face pitchers with xERAs near 5.00, allowing our hitters to capitalize on higher projected contact rates. Additionally, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) remains an auto-start against Canning, buoyed by a strong platoon advantage that keeps his efficiency score well within the elite tier.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s board was the exclusion of **Seiya Suzuki** (57.43) from the starting outfield. Despite his favorable BvP indicators and a boost for facing a slow pitcher\u2014which theoretically aids his stolen base upside\u2014**Seiya Suzuki** was edged out by the superior volume projections of **Henry Bolte** (58.30) and the positional necessity of maintaining our defensive configuration. We are also closely monitoring the status of **Zach McKinstry** (48.79), whose score is highly sensitive to his slot in the order; if he shifts from the #9 hole, we expect a marginal adjustment to his floor. While **Corbin Carroll** (70.40) faces a tougher matchup against May, his elite speed profile keeps him firmly in the lineup as our primary hedge against the league\u2019s scarcity of stolen bases.\n\nWe have consciously lowered the exposure of **Jose Caballero** (42.18) today, as his matchup against Emmet Sheehan is neutralized by a significant platoon disadvantage and a suboptimal spot at the bottom of the order. While **Edwin Arroyo** (57.89) is in the lineup, his high park and SP skill modifiers make him a volatile but necessary play; should the weather in his park turn, we will be forced to reconsider his utility for the remaining games. For now, the roster construction prioritizes high-upside matchups over raw talent to ensure we maintain our competitive advantage in the 5x5 landscape.",
